course: dictionary
lesson: 10942
title: Wag: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and wag is a good example. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits. «A loose, wide wag with a wiggly bottom is usually friendly» is about a dog's tail, so here wag means to move quickly from side to side.
explain: A wag can also be a movement of your finger. If someone wags a finger at you, they are telling you they disagree, not waving hello. In Australia and New Zealand, wag school means skipping school without permission, so the context matters even more.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. If «tongues wag» meant tongues moving, the phrase would make no sense about people. It means people are gossiping. The sense that fits the whole sentence is the right one.
fact: Wag can be a verb (wag, wags, wagged, wagging) or a noun.
fact: Wag means to move quickly from side to side or up and down, especially a dog's tail.
fact: A wagging tail does not always mean a dog is friendly; a stiff, high wag can mean it is nervous or tense.
fact: To wag a finger means to shake a finger at someone to show you disagree.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, wag school means skipping school without permission.
exercise: «A loose, wide wag with a wiggly bottom is usually friendly.» What does wag mean here? | To gossip about someone | To skip school without permission | To shake a finger to show you disagree | *To move quickly from side to side, like a dog's tail
exercise: Dad says, «Don't wag your finger at me, young lady!» What is he telling her? | He is pleased with her | *He does not agree with what she did | He wants her to wave hello | He is asking her to skip school
exercise: In Australia, a child says, «I'm going to wag tomorrow.» What might she mean? | She plans to walk her dog | She plans to spread gossip | *She plans to skip school without permission | She plans to wave at her friends
test: «A stiff, high tail that wags fast while the dog stares can mean the dog is tense.» Does this mean the dog is definitely happy? | Yes, because it is staring at you | *No, a stiff, high wag can mean the dog is tense | No, because dogs do not have tails | Yes, any fast wag means happy
test: What does «tongues wag» mean? | People are fast asleep | People are eating their dinner | *People are gossiping | Tongues move up and down
exam: Mum gave me a wag of her finger when I came home late. What does wag mean here? | *To shake a finger to show she disagreed | To skip school without permission | To wave hello happily | To move a tail from side to side
